.TH CROSS-GPU 1 "2026-11-19" "cross-gpu 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cross-gpu \- cross-platform GPU stress-testing CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cross-gpu
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.B list
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.br
.B cross-gpu
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.B info
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.br
.B cross-gpu
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.B run
[\fB\-\-device\fR \fIall\fR|\fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-test\fR \fITEST\fR|\fIall\fR]
[\fB\-\-duration\fR \fITIME\fR]
[\fB\-\-warmup\fR [\fITIME\fR]]
[\fB\-\-backend\fR \fIauto\fR|\fIPLUGIN_NAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.br
.B cross-gpu \-\-help
.br
.B cross-gpu \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B CrossGPU
is a cross-platform GPU discovery, hardware telemetry, and stress-testing
command-line utility targeting Linux and macOS. It is implemented exclusively
with the Python 3.10+ standard library (zero external Python dependencies) and
loads native GPU runtimes dynamically via
.BR ctypes (3)
using versioned library SONAMEs and standard macOS frameworks.
.PP
Detected physical GPUs are deduplicated across backends using normalized PCIe
BDF addresses, hardware UUIDs or macOS IOKit registry IDs, and ordinal
hardware fingerprints. Native backends (\fBcuda\fR, \fBhip\fR, \fBlevelzero\fR,
\fBmetal\fR) carry priority 100 and take precedence over \fBopencl\fR
(priority 50).
.PP
Output stream separation is strict: \fBstdout\fR carries only formatted
command output (text tables/telemetry, JSON/NDJSON, help, or version), while
\fBstderr\fR carries all errors, discovery diagnostics, compiler logs, and
\fB\-\-verbose\fR traces.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List detected physical GPU devices with active and available backends.
.TP
.B info
Display detailed hardware properties, capability flags, and per-backend
bindings for each detected GPU.
.TP
.B run
Execute GPU stress workloads with optional warmup, golden verification, and
1 Hz hardware telemetry.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-device " all|N"
Target all detected GPUs (\fBall\fR, default) or a single canonical device
index \fIN\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-test " TEST|all"
Workload name, comma-separated workload list, or \fBall\fR (default). Typos
in workload names, backend names, or subcommands produce \fBdifflib\fR
suggestions on \fBstderr\fR and exit with status 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration " TIME"
Timed execution duration per workload using suffix \fBms\fR, \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR,
or \fBh\fR (for example \fB250ms\fR, \fB500ms\fR, \fB60s\fR, \fB5m\fR,
\fB1h\fR). Must be strictly greater than zero. Default: \fB60s\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-warmup " [TIME]"
Warmup duration before each workload using suffix \fBms\fR, \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR,
or \fBh\fR. Defaults to \fB0s\fR when omitted, \fB5s\fR when passed without a
value, or the explicitly specified duration (including \fB0ms\fR / \fB0s\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " auto|PLUGIN_NAME"
Select \fBauto\fR (highest-priority backend per device, default) or a specific
backend plugin name (\fBcuda\fR, \fBhip\fR, \fBlevelzero\fR, \fBmetal\fR,
\fBopencl\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-format " text|json"
Output format (\fBtext\fR default, or \fBjson\fR). In \fBrun\fR mode,
\fBjson\fR emits streaming newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON). Unusable or
unavailable telemetry sensors are omitted from both \fBtext\fR lines and
NDJSON \fBmetrics\fR objects, and ticks with zero usable sensors are suppressed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Convenience shortcut for \fB\-\-format json\fR. Cannot be combined with
\fB\-\-format text\fR. Output on \fBstdout\fR is strictly valid JSON/NDJSON
even when \fB\-\-verbose\fR is enabled.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write structured diagnostic traces exclusively to \fBstderr\fR, covering
shared-library SONAME/framework probes, physical GPU deduplication decisions,
\fBimportlib.resources\fR kernel lookups, cache hits/misses, compile flags, and
compiler diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show command help (including usage examples) and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version information and exit 0.
.SH WORKLOADS
All 20 canonical workloads use fixed SI units (10^9 scaling):
.TP
.B Scalar Integer (GOP/s, GOP)
\fBcompute-int64\fR, \fBcompute-int32\fR, \fBcompute-int16\fR,
\fBcompute-int8\fR, \fBcompute-int4\fR.
.TP
.B Scalar Floating-Point & ML (GFLOP/s, GFLOP)
\fBcompute-fp64\fR, \fBcompute-fp32\fR, \fBcompute-fp16\fR,
\fBcompute-fp8\fR, \fBcompute-fp4\fR, \fBcompute-bf16\fR.
.TP
.B Hardware Matrix / Tensor (GFLOP/s, GOP/s)
\fBcompute-tensor-fp32\fR, \fBcompute-tensor-fp16\fR,
\fBcompute-tensor-bf16\fR, \fBcompute-tensor-int8\fR.
.TP
.B Memory & Bandwidth (GB/s, GB)
\fBmemory-pattern\fR, \fBmemory-sweep\fR, \fBbandwidth-d2d\fR,
\fBbandwidth-h2d\fR, \fBbandwidth-d2h\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit codes are ordered strictly by ascending severity and aggregated across
devices and workloads using \fBmax(codes)\fR:
.TP
.B 0
\fBSUCCESS\fR \- All executed workloads passed without compute or hardware faults.
.TP
.B 1
\fBUSAGE_ERROR\fR \- Invalid CLI arguments, unknown commands/flags/tests/backends,
invalid durations, or conflicting options.
.TP
.B 2
\fBINIT_ERROR\fR \- No compatible GPU/backend found, requested device or
backend unavailable, context creation failed, kernel missing/not registered, or
runtime compilation failed.
.TP
.B 3
\fBCOMPUTE_ERROR\fR \- Numerical/bitwise verification mismatch, silent data
corruption (SDC), unexpected NaN/Inf, or memory pattern/sweep mismatch.
.TP
.B 4
\fBHARDWARE_ERROR\fR \- Device-lost error, GPU hang/reset, uncorrectable ECC
fault, Xid/GPU fault, or fatal thermal fault.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Overrides the base directory for runtime-compiled kernel caches
(stored under \fI$XDG_CACHE_HOME/cross-gpu\fR; defaults to
\fI~/.cache/cross-gpu\fR on Linux and \fI~/Library/Caches/cross-gpu\fR on
macOS unless explicitly set to an absolute path). Ignored when relative per
the XDG specification.
.TP
.B CROSS_GPU_NO_CACHE
When set to a truthy value (\fB1\fR, \fBtrue\fR, \fByes\fR, case-insensitive),
completely bypasses disk cache reads and writes (using in-memory caching only).
.TP
.B CROSS_GPU_CACHE_DIR
Explicitly overrides the runtime-compiled kernel cache directory on any
platform (ignored when \fBCROSS_GPU_NO_CACHE\fR is truthy).
.TP
.B LD_LIBRARY_PATH
Standard Linux dynamic linker search path honored when resolving native GPU
runtime SONAMEs (\fIlibcuda.so.1\fR, \fIlibnvrtc.so.12\fR,
\fIlibamdhip64.so.6\fR, \fIlibze_loader.so.1\fR, \fIlibOpenCL.so.1\fR,
\fIlibnvidia-ml.so.1\fR).
.TP
.B DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH
Standard macOS dynamic linker search path.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/cross-gpu
Default runtime binary cache on Linux (fallback \fI~/.cache/cross-gpu\fR).
.TP
.I ~/Library/Caches/cross-gpu
Default runtime binary cache on macOS (unless \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR is set).
If the cache directory is read-only or unavailable, caching degrades cleanly
to in-memory only.
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm/card*/device/
Linux DRM and \fIhwmon\fR sysfs hierarchy queried for AMD and Intel telemetry.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/cross-gpu
Installed Bash completion script (under \fI$PREFIX/share/...\fR).
.TP
.I /usr/share/zsh/site-functions/_cross-gpu
Installed Zsh completion script (under \fI$PREFIX/share/...\fR).
.TP
.I /usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/cross-gpu.fish
Installed Fish completion script (under \fI$PREFIX/share/...\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
List detected GPUs in JSON format:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cross-gpu list --json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run FP32 compute and memory pattern checks on GPU 0 with 5-second warmup:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cross-gpu run --device 0 --test compute-fp32,memory-pattern --warmup 5s --duration 30s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a 500 ms smoke check with verbose diagnostics routed to stderr:
.PP
.RS
.EX
CROSS_GPU_NO_CACHE=1 cross-gpu run --test compute-fp32 --warmup 250ms --duration 500ms --json -v
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nvidia-smi (1),
.BR rocm-smi (1),
.BR clinfo (1),
.BR ioreg (8)
